<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">North India</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> is the northern region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, including the Indo-Gangetic plain. It has been the heartland of successive empires based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> exploited its political fragmentation and the weakness of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> to found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> in 1526.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/north-india.jpg" alt="North India" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yann; edited by Jim Carter · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taj_Mahal_(Edited).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> is a loosely defined region rather than a fixed administrative unit. It extends from the Himalayan mountain range in the north to the Indo-Gangetic Plain, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thar-desert">Thar Desert</a> and the Central Highlands, and several major rivers cross it, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a>. Official bodies draw its limits differently: the Northern Zonal Council, created in 1956 by the States Reorganisation Act, and the Ministry of Culture's North Culture Zone, established in Patiala on 23 March 1985, include different states.</p><p>Its landscape ranges from the Trans-Himalayan ranges such as the Karakoram, Ladakh and Zanskar, through the Greater Himalayas with Nanda Devi and the Gangotri Glacier, to the forested Shiwalik hills. The plains have a humid subtropical climate with hot summers, cool winters and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> rains, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thar-desert">Thar Desert</a> in the west is arid and can reach temperatures as high as 50 °C.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After 6500 BCE farming and permanent settlements appeared at Mehrgarh and other sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balochistan">Balochistan</a>, and these developed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a>, which flourished from 2500 to 1900 BCE. Kalibangan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rakhigarhi">Rakhigarhi</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a> were among its prominent cities. The region was later the centre of the Vedic culture, the Mahajanapadas, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a> and the Pratihara dynasty.</p><p>In 1206 Qutub-ud-din Aibak declared his independence and founded the Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, which came to control much of North India. By repulsing Mongol raiders in the 13th century it spared India the devastation suffered in West and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, and refugees from those lands brought about a syncretic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-islamic-culture">Indo-Islamic culture</a> in the north.</p><p>By the early 18th century Mughal authority had weakened, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> asserted control in its place. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> took much of North India by the early 19th century, annexing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1803 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Oudh</a> in 1856. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partition-of-india">partition of India</a> caused upheaval and violence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and Delhi. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputana-agency">Rajputana Agency</a> were merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> in 1949, and in 1966 Punjab was reorganised along linguistic lines, creating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh">Himachal Pradesh</a> and the present Punjab. Uttarakhand was carved out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a> in 2000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The people of North India mostly belong to the Indo-Aryan ethno-linguistic branch, with Tibeto-Burman groups along the Himalayan states. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> is the dominant religion, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> is the homeland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhism</a>. The region holds the Hindu pilgrimage centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, Haridwar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a>, the Buddhist sites of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarnath">Sarnath</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushinagar">Kushinagar</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Golden Temple</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a>, as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Sites</a> including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taj-mahal">Taj Mahal</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-fort">Red Fort</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatehpur-sikri">Fatehpur Sikri</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutb-minar">Qutb Minar</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khajuraho">Khajuraho</a> temples. The composite culture produced by contact between Hindu and Muslim traditions is known as Ganga-Jamuni tehzeeb.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “North India” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He took advantage of the political fragmentation of <b>North India</b> and the weakness of the Delhi Sultanate, and his field artillery and mobile cavalry tactics were new to Indian warfare and enabled him to overcome numerically superior forces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Slavery became the predominant system of <b>North India</b> in the 13th century, André Wink writes, and kept considerable importance in the 14th.</blockquote><a href="/societies/delhi-sultanate">Social Hierarchy of the Delhi Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
